program            = 004_case_ranges
area               = 08_gcc_extensions
description        = GCC case ranges (case LOW ... HIGH:) including negative, character, degenerate single-value and fallthrough ranges
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_audit_flags     = -Wall -Wextra -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror
ub_notes           <<END
UB-GATE DEVIATION AND ITS REASON. Every program in area 08_gcc_extensions drops
-pedantic from the default warning gate. Reason: the constructs under test are
non-standard GCC extensions by definition, and -pedantic exists precisely to reject
them; retaining it would fail every program in the area for doing exactly what it is
meant to do. This is the only area-wide deviation in the corpus. No other warning is
relaxed: -Wall, -Wextra, -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow and -Werror are all
retained, and this program is clean under them at -O0, -O1 and -O2. Measured with
gcc 13.3.0 and re-verified on this branch with gcc 13.4.0. The canonical record of this
deviation is the matching paragraph of impl_defined_notes below, which is the one field
this suite reads a gate reason from; it is restated here so that the whole argument for
this program's fitness as test material can be read in one place.

UB-FREEDOM ARGUMENT. Every value is a small int; the largest computation is 1 + 10, far
from INT_MIN and INT_MAX, so there is no signed overflow. No shift operator is used. No
pointer, array or aggregate is used, so there is no aliasing violation and no
one-past-end pointer. Every object is initialized before it is read. No object is
modified twice between sequence points. Every printf call receives at most one
side-effecting argument. Nothing depends on padding bytes or on the addresses of
unrelated objects. Every switch has a default label, so control flow is total for every
possible operand value, and the case ranges within any one switch never overlap, so no
label is ambiguous. The sweep loop runs from -25 to 35 inclusive and always terminates.

Every switch operand is int, never plain char. The character constants used as range
bounds have type int in C. This is deliberate: char was measured as signed on x86_64 and
i686 but unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64, so a plain-char operand could change which
range matches on different targets and manufacture a divergence caused by the test rather
than by the compiler.

The deliberate fallthrough between two case ranges carries a /* fall through */ comment,
which GCC recognizes to suppress -Wimplicit-fallthrough; -Wextra enables that warning and
-Werror makes it fatal, so the comment is required for the gate to pass.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
UB-GATE DEVIATION AND ITS REASON. Every program in area 08_gcc_extensions drops
-pedantic from the default warning gate. Reason: the constructs under test are
non-standard GCC extensions by definition, and -pedantic exists precisely to reject
them; retaining it would fail every program in the area for doing exactly what it is
meant to do. This is the only area-wide deviation in the corpus. No other warning is
relaxed: -Wall, -Wextra, -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow and -Werror are all
retained, and this program is clean under them at -O0, -O1 and -O2. Measured with
gcc 13.3.0 and re-verified on this branch with gcc 13.4.0. A gate deviation is not a
divergence: the gate is a property of the TEST MATERIAL, it is driven by the reference
compiler only, and it renders no verdict about bcc.

Nothing implementation-defined is observed, so no target restriction is needed and all
four targets are compared. Only int values are printed, so the measured width differences
cannot be reached: sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 on x86_64, aarch64
and riscv64. Plain char is never used as a switch operand or a printed value, so the
measured char-signedness difference cannot be reached. No address or pointer value is
printed.

Case range syntax requires a space on both sides of the ellipsis. Measured with
gcc 13.3.0 and re-verified with gcc 13.4.0: writing case 1...5: without spaces produces
"error: too many decimal points in number", because the lexer reads 1. followed by ..5 as
a malformed floating constant. Every range in the program keeps the spaces.

The runtime probes use volatile int operands so the optimizer cannot fold the switch away
and must emit a genuine lowering. Dense and sparse label sets select different lowering
strategies, a jump table versus a comparison chain, and only the runtime variant
exercises them.

Output is identical on all four targets at all three optimization levels.

THIS PROGRAM CARRIES NO EXPECTED-DIVERGENCE MARKER, AND THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN AN
OMISSION. Case ranges are the one construct in the user's mandated extension list that no
documented bcc extension inventory names, and an earlier revision of this record therefore
carried a marker, XD-GCCEXT-CASE-RANGES-001, whose class was compile_failure and whose
basis was that inventory's failure to mention the feature. That marker was withdrawn, and
the marker contract was tightened so that neither it nor anything shaped like it can be
minted again. Two independent reasons, either of which is sufficient:

FIRST, ITS BASIS WAS AN OMISSION, AND AN OMISSION AUTHORISES NOTHING. "The documented
inventory does not list case ranges" is equally consistent with the feature working and
the inventory being incomplete, with the feature not working, and with nobody having
considered the question. A marker built on it converts a real compiler defect into an
expected divergence on the authority of a document that never addressed the subject. The
contract now requires a basis to QUOTE the sentence that states the limitation, and the
register audit resolves that quotation against the cited document's own bytes -- a test an
omission cannot pass, because there is no sentence to quote.

SECOND, NO DIVERGENCE HAD BEEN OBSERVED. The marker described a rejection that bcc was
expected to produce, not one anybody had seen; this branch carries the corpus and no bcc
binary, so no verdict about bcc existed for these cells at all. A marker minted from a
prediction inverts the burden of proof: the run stops reporting the divergence precisely
because somebody guessed it would occur, and a guess that happens to be right is
indistinguishable in the report from one that was never tested. The contract now requires
a captured observation -- the exact command, the exit status, the output, the toolchain and
the date -- and refuses text phrased as an expectation.

WHAT HAPPENS INSTEAD, AND WHY IT IS THE CORRECT OUTCOME. Per constraint C3 the program is
written and executed exactly as before: the feature is exercised, never silently excluded,
and all three oracles remain enabled across all four targets and all three optimization
levels. If bcc rejects the syntax, the divergence is reported as a FINDING, which is
precisely what requirement 6 asks for -- a minimized reproducer with exact reproduction
commands, delivered rather than excused. If bcc accepts it and computes a wrong result,
that too is a FINDING. Either way a maintainer receives evidence instead of an
excuse, and the open question that motivated the marker -- whether the inventory's silence
is an implementation gap or a documentation gap -- is answered by the finding rather than
assumed by the record. Should the repository later STATE the limitation in prose, a marker
may be admitted then, on that sentence, with the divergence captured; the procedure is
recorded in tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md.

WHAT SHAPE OF BASIS WOULD SUFFICE, RECORDED SO THE NEXT AUTHOR DOES NOT HAVE TO REDERIVE IT.
A review of this program proposed grounding the marker on two AFFIRMATIVE statements rather
than on the inventory's silence: docs/project-guide.md line 206, whose Compliance and
Quality Review row records a passing verdict over an enumerated set of parsed constructs,
and line 323, whose Production Readiness Assessment states the implementation is
feature-complete with remaining work "exclusively validation, testing, and packaging" and
"no core implementation gaps exist". Read together those two do assert what the frontend
parses AND that nothing outside it is pending, which is a materially stronger footing than
an omission and is the kind of sentence the contract's quotation test can resolve. It is
recorded here as the standard a future basis must meet, not as one in force: on its own it
still supplies no captured observation, and the contract requires both halves.

WHAT ORACLE SCOPE SUCH A MARKER WOULD HAVE TO CARRY. A build refusal produces no artifact,
so it denies the reference comparison, the cross-backend comparison against the x86-64
baseline and the golden-record assertion their subject at the same instant. A refusal
marker scoped to one arm would excuse that arm and leave the other two reporting the same
documented refusal as findings -- measured at up to twenty-one redundant finding
directories for one documented fact. The contract therefore requires a refusal class to
scope EVERY oracle it blocks, and the parser enforces it rather than trusting the author;
the rule is stated in tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md section 8.5 step 3, in the
suite contract's marker-block section, and in the classifier's own scope documentation.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
cr_bucket_neg=10
cr_bucket_b1=10
cr_bucket_b2=10
cr_bucket_b3=1
cr_bucket_b4=10
cr_bucket_default=20
cr_hex_digit=7
cr_hex_lower=14
cr_hex_upper=11
cr_hex_none=-1
cr_single_hit=42
cr_single_miss=0
cr_fall_low=11
cr_fall_mid=10
cr_fall_high=100
cr_fall_none=0
cr_runtime_bucket=2
cr_runtime_hex=12
END
